WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.460
Hello, I'm Stephanie Flanders.

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:03.090
On today's episode I'll be speaking

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.720 position:59%
with the International Monetary Fund's

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.233
chief economist Gita Gopinath.

00:00:07.233 --> 00:00:09.750
The IMF has just produced
its latest forecast

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:10.770
for the global economy

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.220
and they're pretty bleak.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.180
It's expecting the global economy

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.900 position:58%
to shrink by three percent this year.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.430
That would be the
steepest global reduction

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.660
since the Great Depression,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.590
certainly worse than
anything that happened

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.463
in the global financial crisis.

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:32.310 position:66%
Gita Gopinath, looking at these forecasts,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.234
I noticed that you have
your baseline scenario

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.439
for the global economy

00:00:36.439 --> 00:00:37.830
and then everything else,

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.600
all the alternatives are worse.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.320
What's driving the IMF's forecast

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.490
that we're heading for
this economic contraction

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.070
and why do you think that
all the alternative scenarios

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.293
could be even worse?

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.870
So our baseline assumes

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:55.300
that the pandemic and
the containment measures

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.760
will peak in the second quarter

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.910
for most countries in the world

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.640
and then come off gradually
in the second half.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.165
But clearly that's not a given

00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:04.140
and talking to epidemiologists

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.330
and public health officials,

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.410
there's certainly no
certainty that'll happen.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.400
Now there are some
countries where you are seeing

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.770
containment measures working

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.160 position:59%
and there is a flattening of the curve

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.031
and the number of new
cases are coming down.

00:01:15.031 --> 00:01:16.590
But it's still too early to say,

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.840
which is why we look into
these other scenarios

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.140
where the containment measures

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.426
need to go into the
second half of this year

00:01:23.426 --> 00:01:25.533
and even into 2021.

00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:26.978
And if that happens,

00:01:26.978 --> 00:01:31.540 position:63%
then it will be doubling of the downturn

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:32.949
which is from three percent,

00:01:32.949 --> 00:01:37.310
a negative three percent to
negative six percent in 2020

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.990 position:56%
and almost no recovery then in 2021.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.493
So it would be much worse.

00:01:44.106 --> 00:01:46.520
Does the IMF need new tools

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.820
to deal with this crisis,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.720
help emerging market economies

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.540
get the support they need

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.580
or is it just about scaling
up what you already have?

00:01:55.193 --> 00:01:59.056
At the IMF, we've had
close to 100 countries

00:01:59.056 --> 00:02:03.257
come to us for financing needs.

00:02:03.999 --> 00:02:06.189
Just in the last how long?

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:08.950
Well, in the last four weeks,

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.550
a total of about 100.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.030
So it's a very large number
coming in a very short time.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.260
I don't think we've had 100
countries come to us before.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.080
So this is unprecedented

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.380 position:58%
and then of course the speed at which

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:19.941
is even more unprecedented.

00:02:20.163 --> 00:02:22.877
Globally, we've seen
governments and central banks

00:02:22.877 --> 00:02:24.892
do all they can very quickly

00:02:24.893 --> 00:02:27.021
to limit the damage done

00:02:27.021 --> 00:02:28.925
by the shutdown of the economy

00:02:28.925 --> 00:02:30.625
to defeat the virus.

00:02:30.625 --> 00:02:33.029 position:59%
Despite all that extraordinary effort,

00:02:33.029 --> 00:02:37.275
how worried are you about
the permanent damage

00:02:37.275 --> 00:02:38.942
to the economy and to society

00:02:38.942 --> 00:02:41.246
that might come from this crisis?

00:02:42.142 --> 00:02:44.835
I think there is a
substantial risk in that.

00:02:44.835 --> 00:02:49.248
This is a crisis that's affecting

00:02:49.253 --> 00:02:51.250
small and medium enterprises

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:53.746
along with some of the big ones

00:02:53.752 --> 00:02:56.587
and getting to them is much harder.

00:02:56.592 --> 00:02:59.423 position:66%
There is a lot to be concerned about here.

00:02:59.423 --> 00:03:01.965
Another thing I would want to flag

00:03:01.965 --> 00:03:05.101
is that while in the past
when there's been a crises

00:03:05.101 --> 00:03:07.132
and you have all the
stimulus in the system,

00:03:07.132 --> 00:03:08.689
it is a stimulus,

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:10.790
which means you want
people to go out and spend,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.840
you want folks to invest now,

00:03:12.846 --> 00:03:16.427
but that's not what the
other side of this crisis is,

00:03:16.427 --> 00:03:17.584
which is a health crises.

00:03:17.584 --> 00:03:19.187
And at the same time

00:03:19.187 --> 00:03:20.975 position:58%
all of this stimulus is in the system

00:03:20.975 --> 00:03:22.812
we want people to stay at home

00:03:22.813 --> 00:03:24.940
and not exactly go out and spend.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.741
So it's a very different
transmission channel

00:03:27.741 --> 00:03:28.619
- -

00:03:28.623 --> 00:03:31.560 position:58%
The hope is that you will keep people

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.167
with sufficient income

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:34.973
so that they can meet their needs,

00:03:34.973 --> 00:03:37.461 position:66%
that firms and businesses can stay afloat,

00:03:37.461 --> 00:03:41.523
that once we get past
this period of lockdown,

00:03:41.529 --> 00:03:44.666 position:59%
that things would recover much faster.

00:03:44.666 --> 00:03:46.496
But the economic landscape

00:03:46.505 --> 00:03:47.618
will look very different

00:03:47.643 --> 00:03:49.235
once we come out of this lockdown.

00:03:49.235 --> 00:03:51.067
And I think there is
tremendous uncertainty.

00:03:51.695 --> 00:03:54.281 position:66%
Gita Gopinath, chief economist of the IMF,

00:03:54.281 --> 00:03:55.381
thank you very much.

00:03:55.383 --> 00:03:57.300
Thank you, Stephanie.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.851
on inequality and the
economic cost of inequality

00:04:51.851 --> 00:04:53.570
in recent years

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.850
and we've certainly seen
the impact of the crisis,

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:01.466
the big difference between
employees and big companies

00:05:01.466 --> 00:05:04.910
who perhaps have quite
strong employment rights

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.800
and those in the gig economy

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.850
who don't have that job security,

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.800
bearing the brunt of this crisis.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.804
Do you think there'll be a new
focus on those inequalities

00:05:13.804 --> 00:05:15.580
after this crisis?

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:19.820
For countries to come out of
this really grim situation,

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.670
and this is true for all
countries in the world,

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:25.660
it's going to be
important to get everybody

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:30.015
to be able to survive this
crises and to come out whole

00:05:30.015 --> 00:05:31.403
at the other end.

00:05:31.403 --> 00:05:35.716 position:58%
And so having this kind of inequality

00:05:35.716 --> 00:05:40.716 position:66%
which leads to permanent losses of income,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.220
people coming out losing
out of the labor force

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.130
where they can't maintain
basic livelihoods

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:47.060
doesn't help anybody.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:51.260
It doesn't help even from
just a pure world perspective.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:52.510
So I think absolutely,

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:54.580 position:64%
I think it's very important for the world

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:58.870 position:66%
to ensure that there are again I would say

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:00.370
automatic systems in place

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.470
that get triggered whenever
something like this happens

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:05.350
to ensure that people get
the support that they need

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:06.750
and also to make sure

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.319
that in normal times,

00:06:08.319 --> 00:06:11.871 position:61%
that there is an equitable distribution

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.965
of income in the world.

